WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.120
Have you ever wondered what movies were filling
seats at the movie theater around the time

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.120
of your birth?

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.360
Well, if you were born in 1970 or later, we've
got the answers to which film brought in the

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.320
biggest crowds as you were making your big
entrance.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.010
Here are the biggest hits at the American
box office released each and every year from

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.510
1970 to 2016.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.900
1970 - Love Story

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.650
The aptly-titled Love Story is based on a
popular novel by Erich Segal, and stars Ryan

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.210
O'Neal and Ali MacGraw s the movie's central
couple.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:33.740
This tale of love and loss was the biggest
hit of 1970, with a box-office haul of $106.3

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:35.120
million.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.060
1971 - The French Connection

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:42.070
William Friedkin's cop thriller The French
Connection has often been imitated, but never

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.070
replicated.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:47.490
Gene Hackman won an Oscar for his role, the
movie was named Best Picture, and audiences

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:48.680
couldn't get enough.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.440
It made more than any other 1971 movie, earning
$51.7 million..

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.770
1972 - The Godfather

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:01.570
Francis Ford Coppola's The Godfather is regarded
as one of the most superb films of all time.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.290
Film legend Marlon Brando is captivating,
and it made stars out of cast members like

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.380
Al Pacino and Diane Keaton.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.869
Fans lined up around the block to see the
three-hour film again and again, which made

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:12.720
it the most popular film of 1972.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.280
"That's a terrific story."

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.940
1973 - The Exorcist

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.469
Based on William Peter Blatty's creepy book,
The Exorcist is about a girl who gets possessed

00:01:22.469 --> 00:01:24.549
by a demon named Pazuzu.

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:29.090
Audiences wanted to be terrified in 1973,
and this film did it so well that it topped

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.070
the annual box office chart.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.569
1974 - Blazing Saddles

00:01:33.569 --> 00:01:38.340
Mel Brooks' parody of old Westerns and cowboy
movies is a brutally funny satire focusing

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.869
on themes of prejudice and racism, and it
breaks the fourth wall when the big confrontation

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:45.429
spills out into a present-day studio lot.

00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:49.270
Blazing Saddles offered something for everyone,
which is why it was the biggest theatrical

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.380
draw of 1974.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.030
1975 - Jaws

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.299
It's rare for a horror movie to grab a Best
Picture nomination at the Oscars — but Jaws

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:03.369
did just that, thanks to memorable performances
from Richard Dreyfuss, Robert Shaw, and Roy

00:02:03.369 --> 00:02:07.189
Scheider, all under the watchful eye of young
director Steven Spielberg.

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:12.290
Jaws became not only the most popular movie
of 1975, but the highest-grossing movie ever

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:15.930
— a title it held for just a couple of years.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:16.930
1976 - Rocky

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:21.341
At a time when Sylvester Stallone was nearly
broke and unable to make it as an actor, he

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:24.750
chose to write the classic underdog film Rocky
for himself.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.880
The gamble paid off — Rocky won Best Picture
at the Academy Awards, made Stallone into

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:36.519
a bonafide movie star, and was the top-grossing
movie of 1976, earning $117.2 million.

00:02:36.519 --> 00:02:38.900
1977 - Star Wars

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.900
The space opera set a long time ago in a galaxy
far, far away has thrilled and enchanted audiences

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.120
for 40 years now.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.709
Underscored by the music of John Williams
and featuring performances by Mark Hamill,

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:55.069
Harrison Ford, Carrie Fisher, and James Earl
Jones, Star Wars was a nearly perfect film.

00:02:55.069 --> 00:03:01.290
The franchise starter raked in $460.9 million
at the box office, and after its initial run

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.700
it topped Jaws to become the highest-earning
movie ever.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.819
1978 - Grease

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:10.719
The charismatic John Travolta and charming
Australian singer Olivia Newton-John were

00:03:10.719 --> 00:03:14.629
at the front and center of Grease, based on
the smash hit Broadway musical.

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:18.280
Right in the middle of a '50s revival, the
musical demolished its competition with a

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.310
take of $188.7 million.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.239
"Boy, good news really travels fast."

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:26.859
1979 - Kramer vs. Kramer

00:03:26.859 --> 00:03:31.400
Dustin Hoffman and Meryl Streep both won Oscars
for their roles in the powerful domestic drama

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.819
Kramer vs. Kramer.

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:37.540
The film was so provocative that it enjoyed
"must-see" status — making it the top theatrical

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.430
draw of 1979.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.310
1980 - The Empire Strikes Back

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:46.810
The sequel to the most game-changing, beloved,
and cash-generating movie ever was virtually

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:49.049
assured success at the box office.

00:03:49.049 --> 00:03:53.280
After throwing in an epic battle between Darth
Vader and Luke Skywalker in which — spoiler

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.299
alert — Luke finds out Darth Vader is his
dad, The Empire Strikes Back easily won the

00:03:58.299 --> 00:04:02.900
1980 box office with a haul of $290.4 million.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.760
1981 - Raiders of the Lost Ark

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.700
Raiders of the Lost Ark was intended to be
a tribute to the Saturday afternoon matinee

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:14.239
serials that George Lucas and Steven Spielberg
grew up watching — but it leaves those films

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:15.390
in the dust.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:19.900
Riding on the confident shoulders of Harrison
Ford, this wildly imaginative thrill ride

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:26.180
was the highest grossing film released in
1981, earning an impressive $248.1 million.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.990
1982 - E.T.: The Extra-Terrestrial

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:34.090
It's impossible not to burst into tears of
joy during E.T.'s climax, when the Reese's

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:38.690
Pieces-loving alien uses his powers to launch
the bikes of Elliott and his pals into the

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:39.690
air.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:46.800
E.T. appealed to just about everyone, and
it brought in a staggering $435.1 million.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.120
"Oh, God!"

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:54.590
1983 - Return of the Jedi

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.199
The Star Wars trilogy — a series so popular
that it's commonly referred to as just "the

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:02.460
trilogy" — wrapped up spectacularly with
Return of the Jedi.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.860
And just like its predecessors, Jedi was the
biggest blockbuster of the year of its release.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:11.530
Its total box office take: $309.3 million.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.150
1984 - Ghostbusters

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:18.750
Some of the biggest comedy icons of the decade
came together in a very big way in 1984: to

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.270
fight ghosts and other supernatural beings
menacing New York City.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.210
This mix of broad comedy, ghosts, and Bill
Murray-being-Bill Murray made Ghostbusters

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:31.100
a hit, raking in $242.2 million.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:35.140
"They hate this.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:38.720
I like to torture 'em."

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.090
1985 - Back to the Future

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:46.060
A seemingly impossible blend of comedy, sci-fi,
nostalgia, and awkwardness, the Michael J.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:51.650
Fox/Christopher Lloyd classic Back to the
Future was a smash, topping all other 1985

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.620
movies with a $210.6 million haul.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:57.710
1986 - Top Gun

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:02.509
In 1986, Americans were keenly interested
in taking a highway to the danger zone — so

00:06:02.509 --> 00:06:07.949
interested that they shelled out $179.8 million
at the box office.

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:12.730
In what's probably his most famous role, Tom
Cruise played the justifiably cocky lead character

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:16.220
of Top Gun, your dad's favorite movie to this
day.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.430
1987 - Three Men and a Baby

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:23.000
Tom Selleck, Ted Danson, and Steve Guttenberg
play a trio of bachelors whose party-down

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.520
lifestyle is rocked by the arrival of a baby
on their doorstep in the low-key comedy Three

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.710
Men and a Baby.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:35.340
The surprising 1987 champion cruised to a
box office haul of $167.7 million.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.690
1988 - Rain Man

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:41.940
It's not often that the highest-grossing movie
of the year also wins the Academy Award for

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.020
Best Picture.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:47.180
Rain Man, starring Tom Cruise and Dustin Hoffman,
is one of the few to pull it off, bridging

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:53.190
commercial appeal with artistic aims — and
scoring a whopping $172.8 million.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.539
1989 - Batman

00:06:55.539 --> 00:07:00.090
Moviegoers had never seen a comic book film
quite as big or as bold as Batman, which won

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:04.500
1989 by earning $251.1 million.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.510
Directed by Tim Burton, Batman starred the
unlikely choice of Michael Keaton in the title

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:12.740
role — although Jack Nicholson's Clown Prince
of Crime stole the entire movie.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:16.330
"You can call me Joker."

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:18.300
1990 - Home Alone

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.470
You wouldn't think the year's biggest hit
would be a Christmas movie starring child

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:25.970
actor Macaulay Culkin as an abandoned child
violently fending off burglars.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.780
But the funny, fast-moving John Hughes script
made it a classic that's worth revisiting

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:31.129
every holiday season.

00:07:31.129 --> 00:07:36.539
Still the highest-grossing Christmas movie
of all time, Home Alone gathered $285.7 million,

00:07:36.539 --> 00:07:39.409
enough to make it the number one movie of
1990.

00:07:39.409 --> 00:07:42.550
"Keep the change ya filthy animal."

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:46.000
1991 - Beauty and the Beast

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.260
As the lyrics of the title song note, Beauty
and the Beast is a tale as old as time, but

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:55.009
it hadn't ever been presented as beautifully
as Disney's 1991 animated film.

00:07:55.009 --> 00:07:59.210
The delightful and emotional musical became
the first animated movie ever nominated for

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:03.460
Best Picture, and while it didn't win, it
did wind up the highest-grossing movie to

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:05.590
be released in 1991.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.039
1992 - Aladdin

00:08:08.039 --> 00:08:12.780
The late '80s/early '90s Disney animation
renaissance just might have peaked with Aladdin,

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:17.599
the beautifully animated adaptation filled
with memorable songs, fantastic characters,

00:08:17.599 --> 00:08:20.770
and a showstopping performance from Robin
Williams as the Genie.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:26.330
Aladdin was the biggest hit of 1992, bringing
in $217.3 million.

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:28.720
1993 - Jurassic Park

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:32.480
Steven Spielberg thought it would be a good
idea to make a movie about a dinosaur theme

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:36.789
park based on a bestseller by Michael Crichton
using realistically rendered dinosaurs.

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:38.539
He was right, of course.

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:42.550
And let's be honest — it would've been a
shock if Jurassic Park didn't become the highest

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:43.740
grossing movie of the year.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:48.280
The 1993 dino-flick took in $402.4 million.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:52.460
"Clever girl."

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.390
1994 - The Lion King

00:08:55.390 --> 00:09:00.320
A magnificently animated retelling of Hamlet,
The Lion King is a beautiful and emotional

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.110
film, underscored by original music by Elton
John.

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:07.840
The hit musical was the third Disney animated
feature in a row to top the annual box office,

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:10.560
earning $422 million.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.970
1995 - Toy Story

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:17.649
As the first all-CGI cartoon feature, Pixar's
Toy Story attracted viewers who wanted to

00:09:17.649 --> 00:09:20.020
check out the next generation of animation.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:23.870
But what really brought in the $191.7 million?

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:27.240
Its premise — that toys come alive when
humans aren't around — and its resonant

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.589
themes of friendship and acceptance.

00:09:29.589 --> 00:09:32.010
1996 - Independence Day

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:35.890
Who wouldn't want to see a bunch of Americans
from different walks of life team up to defeat

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:37.620
evil hordes of alien invaders?

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.250
Also, the aliens blew up the White House and
other landmarks, which was pretty awesome

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.660
and realistic-looking in 1996.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:49.029
Independence Day, led by Will Smith, took
in a whopping $306.1 million.

00:09:49.029 --> 00:09:51.370
1997 - Titanic

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:55.730
At the time, James Cameron's historical romantic
drama set aboard the doomed ocean-liner was

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.680
the most expensive movie ever made.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.570
It won 11 Academy Awards, turned Leonardo
DiCaprio into a household name, and became

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:06.630
the first movie to earn $600 million at the
domestic box office.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:11.670
That made Titanic not only the highest-grossing
movie released in 1997, but the highest-grossing

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.880
movie ever, to that point.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:16.410
1998 - Saving Private Ryan

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:21.010
Steven Spielberg's gut-wrenching World War
II epic Saving Private Ryan, starring Tom

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:25.430
Hanks, was more emotionally and physically
real than any other war film people had seen,

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:28.860
and it brought in $216.5 million.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.589
1999 - Star Wars Episode 1: The Phantom Menace

00:10:32.589 --> 00:10:37.220
The first of three Star Wars prequels, The
Phantom Menace was probably the most anticipated

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:38.500
movie of all time.

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:42.980
When it finally played to packed houses in
May 1999, many were vocally disappointed.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:46.950
A lot of people may not have liked it, but
just about everybody saw it — and it pulled

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:50.140
in a whopping $474.5 million.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.990
And the rest…

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:56.790
Movies have gotten bigger and flashier ever
since the new millennium hit, so one can only

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:58.970
imagine what the future of cinema holds.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.650
From the How The Grinch Stole Christmas to
Avatar to Rogue One, here are all the rest

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:04.190
of the biggest box-office hits, from 2000
to 2016.

00:12:31.639 --> 00:12:32.639
Thanks for watching!

00:12:32.639 --> 00:12:34.990
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:37.260
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

